Within the framework of a Friedmann–Lemaître–Robertson–Walker cosmology, let the present-day density parameters be Ω_m (matter), Ω_Λ (cosmological constant or dark-energy term in units of the critical density ρ_c = 3H_0²/(8πG)), with H_0 the present Hubble constant. The dimensionless quantity Ω_Λ = Λ c²/(3H_0²) is commonly inferred from the Type Ia supernova (SN Ia) Hubble diagram (apparent magnitude versus redshift), with a fiducial flat ΛCDM fit yielding Ω_Λ ≈ 0.7.

Consider the following potential systematic effects on the SN Ia inference of Ω_Λ:
(a) attenuation of distant SN Ia light by a diffuse intergalactic dust component producing a redshift-dependent dimming mimicking acceleration;
(b) a metallicity dependence of the SN Ia peak luminosity, where progenitor metallicity Z varies with cosmic epoch, producing corrections to inferred distance moduli comparable in magnitude to cosmological effects;
(c) host-galaxy extinction with a non-standard reddening law;
(d) competing cosmological observables, such as the angular-size–redshift relation for ultracompact radio sources, which under certain analyses prefer Λ ≤ 0;
(e) the discrepancy of order 10^{120} between Λ inferred observationally and the vacuum energy density predicted by quantum field theory.

Determine whether, after fully accounting for systematics of types (a)–(d) and reconciling with the theoretical constraint (e), the conclusion Ω_Λ ≈ 0.7 (and in particular Ω_Λ > 0) is uniquely fixed by the data, or whether viable cosmological models with substantially different values of Ω_Λ remain consistent with the full body of observations.

